WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:08.560
In the name of God, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. Peace and blessings be upon the most noble of prophets and messengers, our Prophet Muhammad, upon him be the best prayer and complete peace.

00:08.560 --> 00:16.820
Peace be upon you and the mercy of God and His blessings. Welcome to the fourth session of our fellowship, titled Communication and Health and Climate Education.

00:16.820 --> 00:24.420
In this session, we will break down together the principles of effective communication in health and climate education, and how we turn scientific knowledge into practical steps.

00:24.420 --> 00:39.920
The subject is not merely an information lesson. We are here to make real and tangible change in people's lives. Let us enter directly into the subject: Imagine that you put in great effort and prepared an educational session that is scientifically accurate by 100 percent.

00:39.920 --> 00:52.260
The information is correct and the numbers are down to the millimeter. But after just one week, you are surprised that no one remembered anything, nor did any behavior change. A frustrating thing, right? This raises the fundamental question:

00:52.260 --> 01:05.040
Is merely having correct information enough for people's condition to change? To solve this problem, our roadmap in this explanation is divided into six essential stations. We will start

01:05.040 --> 01:15.060
with the first station, which is the dilemma of wasted information: Does the precision of information alone suffice? Let us be agreed: Success in health

01:15.060 --> 01:22.240
and climate education is never measured by the quantity of information we memorize in our minds. Real success is our ability to make the person in front of us

01:22.240 --> 01:30.700
understand the information, remember it, and feel confident while applying it in their daily life. There is a difference like heaven and earth between throwing words in one direction

01:30.700 --> 01:38.680
and engaging people in effective communication. Always remember: if your message is accurate but did not arrive, the problem is usually not

01:38.680 --> 01:46.780
in people's awareness. The problem is in the method and channel you chose to deliver it. This brings us to the second station: The loop of real

01:46.780 --> 01:52.220
communication, composed of five elements. Proper communication is a two-way loop. Simply:

01:52.220 --> 01:58.040
From the sender, then the message via the medium or channel to the receiver, and finally

01:58.040 --> 02:04.400
the most important element: feedback. The concept of someone holding a microphone and speaking in one direction was never

02:04.400 --> 02:11.600
communication. Real communication is a living exchange of ideas and even feelings. Feedback is what transforms speech from

02:11.600 --> 02:19.580
a mere lecture into real and effective dialogue. Unfortunately, this is the element most forgotten and lost by us in the field. We enter the third station:

02:19.580 --> 02:22.200
Simple language and active listening.

02:22.220 --> 02:29.720
These are the first two principles of effective communication. The first principle: Speak in their simple language. Compare between someone

02:29.720 --> 02:37.940
who says: "An elevation in particulate matter increases the inflammatory response in the bronchioles" (which builds a wall between you and the people), and between someone who says

02:37.940 --> 02:46.020
simply: "Polluted air makes breathing difficult and harms the lungs in the long run." The idea is the same, but simple language delivers the information

02:46.020 --> 02:55.080
smoothly and without the complexity of medical terminology. The second principle: Attentive listening or active listening, because communication is dialogue. Always ask them:

02:55.080 --> 03:03.720
"What is your opinion on this step? What prevents you from applying it?" A simple question like this makes people feel respected and that their voice is heard, opening

03:03.720 --> 03:13.860
their minds to receive your message. Now we go to the fourth station: Building trust and action. Principles three and four: Principle three says: Build trust before

03:13.860 --> 03:22.320
you try to influence. People do not follow advice merely because it represents facts. People follow the person they trust. Trust is built by honesty,

03:22.320 --> 03:33.520
reliable sources, and the courage to say: "I do not know the answer to this question right now, but I will search for it and return to answer you." Trust is built brick by brick patiently, but it can

03:33.520 --> 03:42.360
collapse and be lost in a single second. The fourth principle: Give them practical, achievable steps. Instead of frightening people with heat and high temperature, it is better

03:42.360 --> 03:50.420
to give them practical keys: Drink a cup of water every hour; avoid going out into the sun between 12:00 PM and 4:00 PM; and check on your elderly

03:50.420 --> 04:00.920
neighbors. People need to know exactly what to do to overcome the risk. Now we arrive at the fifth station: Famous communication traps and errors that we must stay completely

04:00.920 --> 04:30.920
away from. First, information overload: When we dump many details all at once, we cause exhaustion and fatigue for the listener. Second, complex terminology: Difficult language creates isolation between you and the people. Third, fear-mongering without solutions: Frightening people with disease and climate without giving them a solution or hope creates paralysis, causing them to ignore the entire message. And ignoring people's questions and fears, which loses their trust in the blink of an eye. Finally, the sixth station: Knowledge versus real change.

04:30.920 --> 05:00.920
This is the fundamental goal of education. Effective communication is the fine thread that separates theoretical knowledge found in books from real change on the ground. Our success as health educators is not in the amount of information in our minds, but in our skill in turning this science into simple, relatable messages that are easy to apply. Now, after learning how to build and deliver an impactful message, we will move in the next part to crucial topics: the ethical duties and professional responsibilities of the health educator, and how to maintain our credibility in the field.

05:00.920 --> 05:25.820
In the end, we leave you with this simple activity: Think of a health or climate message you would like to share with those around you. Who is the target audience? How will you formulate the message in simple, relatable language without complexity? And what is the direct practical action you will ask of them? Applying the steps is the correct beginning for any real impact. Thank you, and we will see you in the next video.
